
### **🔹 Master Algorithm Summary Table**

| *Algorithm*                                   | *Main Idea*                                                                                            | *Advantages*                                                                     | *Disadvantages*                                                        | *Loss / Cost Function*                                                                  |
| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Linear Regression**                         | Fits a straight line to predict continuous values using least squares.                                 | Simple, interpretable, efficient, works well with linear relationships.          | Sensitive to outliers, assumes linearity, poor with non-linear data.   | Mean Squared Error (MSE):  J(\theta) = \frac{1}{2m}\sum (h_\theta(x^{(i)}) - y^{(i)})^2 |
| **Gradient Descent**                          | Iteratively adjusts parameters to minimize cost function.                                              | Works for many models, simple concept, scalable.                                 | Can converge to local minima, sensitive to learning rate α.            | Indirectly minimizes the chosen cost (e.g., MSE for regression).                        |
| **Logistic Regression**                       | Uses the sigmoid function to model binary outcomes; outputs probability between 0 and 1.               | Probabilistic interpretation, simple and fast, good baseline for classification. | Fails for non-linear separability, assumes independence of features.   | Binary Cross-Entropy:  L(h_\theta(x),y) = -y\log h_\theta(x) - (1-y)\log(1-h_\theta(x)) |
| **Decision Trees (ID3)**                      | Recursively splits data by attributes that maximize information gain (entropy reduction).              | Easy to interpret, handles non-linear relations, robust to outliers.             | Overfits easily, biased toward features with many values.              | Entropy-based Information Gain:  \text{Entropy}(S) = -p_+\log_2p_+ - p_-\log_2p_-       |
| **Random Forests**                            | Ensemble of decision trees using bagging and feature randomness.                                       | Reduces overfitting, robust to noise, good accuracy.                             | Computationally heavy, less interpretable.                             | Sum of tree-level entropy or Gini impurity across ensemble.                             |
| **K-Means Clustering**                        | Partitions data into K clusters by minimizing within-cluster variance.                                 | Simple, fast, easy to implement.                                                 | Requires K, sensitive to initialization, fails on non-convex clusters. | Sum of Squared Error (SSE):  $begin:math:text$ J = \sum_i                               |
| **DBSCAN (Density-Based Spatial Clustering)** | Groups points by density; defines clusters as dense regions separated by sparse regions.               | Finds arbitrary-shaped clusters, robust to noise, no need to predefine K.        | Struggles with varying densities and high-dimensional data.            | No explicit loss; objective is maximizing density-connectedness .                       |
| **PCA (Principal Component Analysis)**        | Reduces dimensionality by projecting data to directions (principal components) that maximize variance. | Simplifies models, removes noise, visualizes high-D data.                        | Loses interpretability, only captures linear structure.                | Variance maximization equivalent to minimizing projection error \|x - (x^Tu)u\|^2 .     |
| **Regularization (L2/Ridge)**                 | Penalizes large coefficients to reduce overfitting.                                                    | Prevents overfitting, keeps all features.                                        | May underfit if λ too large.                                           | J(\theta) = \frac{1}{2m}\sum (h_\theta(x^{(i)}) - y^{(i)})^2 + \lambda\sum \theta_j^2   |
| **Cross-Validation (K-Fold)**                 | Evaluates model generalization by rotating train/test splits.                                          | Better performance estimate, uses all data.                                      | Computationally expensive.                                             | No loss function (validation strategy).                                                 |
| **Neural Networks (Feedforward / MLP)**       | Layers of neurons apply weighted sums + nonlinear activations to approximate complex functions.        | Handles nonlinearities, learns feature hierarchies, flexible architecture.       | Requires large data, high compute cost, risk of overfitting.           | Typically Cross-Entropy or MSE depending on task  .                                     |

