### **🔹 MLP (Multilayer Perceptron) — Detailed Section**

| *Aspect*                               | *Description*                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |
| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Main Idea**                          | An MLP consists of multiple fully connected layers; each neuron computes a weighted sum of inputs followed by a nonlinear activation (e.g., ReLU, sigmoid, tanh). It learns representations layer by layer to map complex input-output relations. |
| **Needs**                              | Large labeled datasets, non-linear activation functions (ReLU, tanh, sigmoid), proper weight initialization, and sufficient computational power (GPU).                                                                                            |
| **Advantages**                         | - Learns non-linear functions- Flexible for regression/classification- Universal function approximator- Scalable to deep architectures                                                                                                            |
| **Disadvantages**                      | - Computationally expensive- Requires large data- Prone to overfitting- Opaque (black-box) decisions                                                                                                                                              |
| **Loss Function (for Classification)** | **Binary Cross-Entropy:**  L = -y\log(\hat{y}) - (1-y)\log(1-\hat{y})  — used when output activation = Sigmoid.                                                                                                                                   |
| **Loss Function (for Regression)**     | **Mean Squared Error (MSE):**  L = \frac{1}{2m}\sum (h_\theta(x^{(i)}) - y^{(i)})^2.                                                                                                                                                              |
| **Loss Function Advantages**           | - Cross-Entropy provides stronger gradients for probabilistic classification.- MSE provides smooth optimization for continuous outputs.                                                                                                           |
| **Loss Function Disadvantages**        | - Cross-Entropy can explode with poor initialization.- MSE less effective for imbalanced classification and non-binary tasks.                                                                                                                     |

